  "step_2": "The individual first considered the size of the winnings between A and B, then turned to the probabilities. The individual notes that B offers a 25% chance to win 3000€, which is higher than A’s 20% chance to win 4000€. The individual finds B more attractive because the probability of winning is higher, even though the amount is lower by 1000€. The justification centers on the higher chance of winning in B (25% vs 20%) outweighing the smaller prize. The individual does not mention skewness explicitly, but focuses on the probability of a favorable outcome (winning something) as a key factor in the decision. The reasoning emphasizes the likelihood of a non-zero outcome as a dominant consideration.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on comparing the degree to which each lottery concentrates probability on favorable (non-zero) outcomes — that is, the skewness toward gains. The individual considers exactly this: the probability of winning something (favorable outcome) versus getting nothing. The individual explicitly prefers B because it has a higher chance of a non-zero outcome (25% vs 20%), which aligns with preferring the more left-skewed lottery. Although the term 'skewness' is not used, the cognitive process — prioritizing the probability of a gain over the magnitude — matches the rule of the decision reason. The information considered (probabilities of winning) and the justification (higher chance of winning makes B more attractive) are consistent with the skewness-based rule. Therefore, it is highly likely the individual used a reasoning process equivalent to the stated decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 95
}

